<section class="mt-12 max-w-3xl space-y-5">
  <p>
    There is a category of letter that most people have never written and
    which is worth real money: the formal demand, the appeal, the request
    for validation. They follow conventions, they cite the right things, and
    they are unmistakably from somebody who intends to follow through.
  </p>
  <p>
    Nobody is ever taught to write one. So mostly they don't get written,
    and the deposit stays unreturned.
  </p>
</section>

<section class="mt-14">
  <h2 class="display text-2xl">What you give it, and what you get</h2>
  <ol class="mt-6 space-y-4">
    <li class="card p-5">
      <h3 class="font-semibold">1 · A line of facts</h3>
      <p class="mt-1.5 text-sm text-soft">
        "Moved out June 30th, £1,800 deposit, 12 Oak Street, no itemised
        list yet." Written how you'd say it. If the relevant document is
        already in your vault, it'll read that too.
      </p>
    </li>
    <li class="card p-5">
      <h3 class="font-semibold">2 · A letter you can send</h3>
      <p class="mt-1.5 text-sm text-soft">
        Formal, specific, unemotional, and structured the way these letters
        are structured — stating what it is in the first line, making the
        demand plainly, and setting a response date.
      </p>
    </li>
    <li class="card p-5">
      <h3 class="font-semibold">3 · The follow-through, scheduled</h3>
      <p class="mt-1.5 text-sm text-soft">
        The response date on your calendar and the next steps on your list.
        A letter nobody follows up on is a letter that gets ignored, and
        this is the half people drop.
      </p>
    </li>
  </ol>
</section>

<section class="mt-14 max-w-3xl">
  <h2 class="display text-2xl">Blanks, not inventions</h2>
  <p class="mt-4 text-soft">
    Where it doesn't know something — an address, an account number, a date
    you didn't give it — you get a clearly marked blank to fill in. It never
    guesses a plausible detail, because a letter with an invented fact in it
    is worse than no letter: it hands the other side a reason to dismiss
    everything else in it.
  </p>
</section>

<section class="mt-14 max-w-3xl">
  <h2 class="display text-2xl">Hedged where the law lives</h2>
  <p class="mt-4 text-soft">
    Deadlines and penalties depend on where you are and change over time. So
    the letters say a deadline is <em>commonly</em> a certain length and
    that you should verify the rules for your area — even when the app knows
    roughly where you live. A model confidently citing a statute is a model
    confidently citing a statute it may have made up.
  </p>
</section>

<section class="mt-14 max-w-3xl">
  <h2 class="display text-2xl">You send it</h2>
  <p class="mt-4 text-soft">
    Nothing is sent for you. The letter is a draft in your own notes, in
    your name, which you read, edit and post yourself. An assistant that
    fired off formal legal correspondence on your behalf would be a much
    worse idea than it first sounds.
  </p>
</section>
